== Chapter VII

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[The captivating power of indwelling sin, and what it consists in. The prevailing power of sin, when it is from itself, and when from temptation. The rage and madness that is in sin.]
]

#smallcaps[The] third thing ascribed to this #emph[law of sin] in its opposition to God and the law of his grace is that it #emph[takes the soul captive] (Rom 7:23): “I see another law … holding me captive (making me captive) to the law of sin.” This is the utmost height to which the apostle in that place carries the opposition and warring of the remainders of indwelling sin. He closes his consideration of it with a complaint of the state and condition of believers because of it, and an earnest prayer for deliverance from it (verse 24): “What a wretched man I am! Who will rescue me from this body of death?” What is contained in this expression and meant by it will be declared in the following observations.

First, it is not directly the #emph[power] and actions of the law of sin that are expressed here, but its #emph[success] in and by its actions. But #emph[success] is the greatest evidence of power, and #emph[taking captive] in war is the height of #emph[success]. No one can aim at greater #emph[success] than to take his enemies captive. And it is a particular expression in the Scripture for #emph[great success]. So the Lord Christ, on his victory over Satan, is said to have “led captivity captive” (Eph 4:8); that is, to have conquered him who had conquered and prevailed over others. And he did this when by his death he “destroyed him who holds the power of death, that is, the devil” (Heb 2:14). Here, then, a great #emph[prevailing power] and strength of sin in its warring against the soul is revealed. It wars so as to #emph[take captive]. It could not do this if it did not have great power, especially against the #emph[resistance] of the soul that is included in this expression.

Secondly, it is said to take the soul captive “to the law of sin”: not to this or that sin, a particular sin, an actual sin, but to the #emph[law of sin]. For the most part God orders things so, and gives out such supplies of grace to believers, that they are not made a prey to #emph[this] or #emph[that] particular sin. He does not allow it to prevail in them and compel them to serve it in its lusts, to have dominion over them, so that they are its #emph[captives] and slaves. This is what #emph[David] prays against so earnestly in Ps 19:12, 13: “Cleanse me from my hidden faults. Keep Your servant also from presumptuous sins; may they not rule over me. Then I will be blameless.” He supposes that the law of sin continues in him (verse 12), which will bring forth #emph[errors of life] and secret sins; and against these he finds relief in pardoning and cleansing mercy, which he prays for. “This,” he says, “will be my condition. But as for #emph[sins of pride] and boldness, such as all sins are that get #emph[dominion] in a man and make a #emph[captive] of him, Lord, restrain your servant from them.” For whatever sin gets such power in a man, whether it is small or great in its own nature, becomes in the man in whom it is a sin of #emph[boldness, pride], and presumption. For these things are not reckoned from the nature or kind of the sin, but from its prevailing power and habitual practice, in which its pride, boldness, and contempt of God consist. #emph[Jabez] prays to the same purpose, if I am not mistaken (1 Chr 4:10): “If only You would bless me and enlarge my territory! May Your hand be with me and keep me from evil, so that it may not grieve me.” The holy man took occasion from his #emph[own name] to pray against sin, that it might not be a #emph[grief and sorrow] to him by its power and prevailing. I admit that it may sometimes come to this with a believer: for a time he may be #emph[taken captive] by some particular sin. It may have so much prevailing power #emph[in] him as to have power #emph[over] him. So it seems to have been with #emph[David] when he lay so long in his sin without repentance. And it was plainly so with those in Isa 57:17, 18: “I was enraged by his sinful greed, so I struck him and hid My face in anger; yet he went on stubbornly in the way of his heart. I have seen his ways, but I will heal him.” They continued under the power of their #emph[covetousness], so that none of God’s dealings with them for so long a time could reclaim them. But for the most part, when any lust or sin prevails in this way, it is from the advantage and help it has gained from some #emph[powerful temptation] of Satan. He has poisoned it, inflamed it, and entangled the soul. So the apostle, speaking of those who had fallen away from their holiness through sin, says they were in “the snare of the devil, who has taken them captive to his will” (2 Tim 2:26). Though it was their own lust that they served, they were brought into bondage to it by being entangled in some snare of Satan. And so they are said to be #emph[taken alive], like a poor beast in a trap.

And here, in passing, we may inquire a little whether the prevailing power of a particular sin in anyone comes from itself or from the influence of #emph[temptation] on it. On this, take only these two observations for the present.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, much of the #emph[prevailing power] of sin over the soul certainly comes from #emph[Satan] when the perplexing and captivating sin has no particular footing or advantage in the nature, constitution, or condition of the sinner. When any #emph[lust] grows high and prevails more than others on its own account, it is from the particular advantage it has in the natural constitution of the person, or in his station or condition in the world. For otherwise the law of sin gives an equal inclination to all evil and an equal vigor to every lust. So when it cannot be discerned that the #emph[captivating] sin is particularly fixed in the nature of the sinner, or is helped by his upbringing or his work in the world, its prevailing power comes particularly from Satan. He has got to its root and has given it poison and strength. Indeed, perhaps at times what may seem to the #emph[soul] to be the corrupt #emph[lusting] of the heart is nothing but Satan forcing his suggestions on the imagination. If, then, a man finds a persistent rage from some corruption that is not evidently seated in his nature, let him, as the Papists say, #emph[cross] himself, or flee by faith to the cross of Christ, for the devil is close at hand.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, when a lust prevails to #emph[captivity] where it brings in no advantage to the #emph[flesh], it is from Satan. All that the law of sin does of itself is to serve the #emph[provision of the flesh] (Rom 13:14), and it must bring in to the flesh something of the profits and pleasures that are its object. Now if the prevailing sin does not act in this way, if it is more spiritual and inward, it comes much more from Satan, by way of the imagination, than from the corruption of the heart itself. But this is in passing.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I say, then, that the apostle is not dealing here with our being #emph[taken captive] to this or that sin, but to the #emph[law of sin]. That is, we are compelled to bear its presence and burden whether we want to or not. At times the soul thinks or hopes that through grace it may be #emph[utterly freed] from this troublesome lodger. After some sweet enjoyment of God, some full supply of grace, some return from wandering, some deep affliction, some thorough humiliation, the poor soul begins to hope that it will now be freed from the law of sin. But after a while it sees that it is quite otherwise. Sin acts again and holds its old position, and the soul finds that whether it wants to or not, it must bear its yoke. This makes it sigh and cry out for deliverance.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, this taking captive shows a prevailing against the #emph[resistance], or the contrary actions, of the will. This is plainly implied in this expression, namely, that the will opposes, and as it were rises up against, the working of sin. The apostle declares this in the expressions he uses in verses 15, 19, 20. And this is what the #emph[lusting of the Spirit] against the flesh consists in (Gal 5:17), that is, the contending of grace to drive it out and subdue it. The spiritual habits of grace that are in the #emph[will] resist it and act against it in this way, and the stirring up of those habits by the Spirit is directed to the same purpose. This taking #emph[captive], I say, is contrary to the inclinations and actions of the #emph[renewed will]. No man is made a captive except against his will. Captivity is misery and trouble, and no man willingly puts himself into trouble. Men choose it in its #emph[causes], and in the ways and means that lead to it, but not in itself. So the prophet tells us in Hos 5:11: “Ephraim was not willingly oppressed and crushed in judgment”; that was his misery and trouble. But he willingly followed the command of the #emph[idolatrous kings], which brought him to it. Whatever consent, then, the soul may give to sin, which is the means of this captivity, it gives none to the #emph[captivity] itself; that is wholly against the will. From this the following things result.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, #emph[the power of sin is great], which is what we are demonstrating. This appears in its prevailing to captivity against the actions and strivings of the will for liberty from it. If no opposition were made to it, or if its adversary were weak, negligent and lazy, it would be no great evidence of its power that it made captives. But its prevailing against diligence, activity, watchfulness, and the constant resistance of the will proves its efficacy.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, this taking captive implies #emph[many particular successes]. If it had no success in particulars, it could not be said to take captive at all. It might rebel, it might assault, but it cannot be said to #emph[take captive] without some successes. And there are several degrees of the success of the law of sin in the soul. At times it carries the person into outward #emph[actual sin], which is its utmost aim. At times it obtains the #emph[consent of the will], but is cast out by grace and goes no further. At times it wearies and entangles the soul so that it turns aside, as it were, and gives up contending, which is a success too. One or more or all of these must be present where #emph[captivity] takes place. The apostle ascribes this kind of course to covetousness in 1 Tim 6:9.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, this taking captive shows this condition #emph[to be miserable and wretched]. To be yoked and dealt with like this against the judgment of the mind, against the choice and consent of the will, against its utmost strivings and contendings: how sad it is! When the neck is sore and tender from former burdens, to be compelled to bear the yoke again: this pierces, this grieves, this even breaks the heart. The soul has been given by grace a principle of #emph[loathing] sin and every evil way, of hatred for the least disagreement between itself and the holy will of God. Then to be forced by this #emph[law of sin], with all the enmity and folly, the deadness and filth that go with it: what condition could be more dreadful? All captivity is dreadful in its own nature, and the greatest aggravation of it comes from the character of the tyrant to whom anyone is made captive. Now what can be worse than this law of sin? That is why the apostle, having once mentioned this captivity, cries out like one quite weary and ready to faint (verse 24).]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Fourthly, this condition is #emph[peculiar to believers]. Unregenerate men are not said to be #emph[taken captive] to the law of sin. They may indeed be taken captive to this or that particular sin or corruption; that is, they may be forced to serve it against the power of their convictions. They are convinced of its evil (an adulterer of his uncleanness, a drunkard of his abomination), and perhaps they make some resolutions against it. But their lust is too strong for them; they cannot stop sinning, and so they are made captives or slaves to this or that particular sin. But they cannot be said to be #emph[taken captive to the law of sin], and that is because they are willingly subject to it. It has, as it were, a rightful dominion over them, and they do not oppose it, except when it breaks out to the disturbance of their consciences. And then the opposition they make to it does not come from their #emph[wills]\; it is merely the action of a frightened conscience and a convinced mind. They do not regard the #emph[nature] of sin, but its #emph[guilt] and consequences. But to be brought into captivity is what befalls a man against his will. This is all that will be said about this degree of the actions of the power of sin, as it shows itself in its success.]]

=== The Rage and Madness of Sin

The fourth and last degree of the opposition the law of sin makes to God and the law of his will and grace is in its #emph[rage] and #emph[madness]. There is #emph[madness] in its nature (Eccl 9:3): “the hearts of men are full of evil and madness.” The evil that the heart of man is full of by nature is the #emph[indwelling sin] we are speaking of. And it is so in their heart that it rises up to #emph[madness]. The Holy Spirit expresses this rage of sin by a fitting picture which he uses in various places, such as Jer 2:24 and Hos 8:9. It makes #emph[men like a wild donkey]: she roams her ways, sniffs the wind, and runs wherever her mind or lust leads her. And he says of idolaters enraged with their lusts that they “go mad over idols” (Jer 50:38). We may consider a little what lies in this #emph[madness] and rage of sin, and how it rises up to it.

First, as for its #emph[nature], it seems to consist in a violent, headlong, stubborn pressing toward evil or sin. There is #emph[violence], persistence and stubbornness in it. It is the tearing and torturing of the soul by some sin to force its consent and obtain satisfaction. It rises up in the heart, is refused and rebuked by the law of grace. It returns and puts out its poison again. The soul is startled and throws it off. It returns again with new violence and persistence. The soul cries out for help and deliverance, looks around to all the springs of gospel grace and relief, trembles at the furious assaults of sin, and throws itself into the arms of Christ for deliverance. And if it is not able to take that course, it is beaten and dragged up and down through the mire and filth of foolish imaginations and corrupt, foul lusts, which rend and tear it as if they would devour its whole spiritual life and power. See 1 Tim 6:9, 10; 2 Pet 2:14. It was not much different with those we gave as an instance before (Isa 57:17). They had an inflamed, enraged lust working in them, namely #emph[covetousness], or the love of this world, by which, as the apostle speaks, men “pierce themselves with many sorrows.” God is angry with them and shows his wrath by all the ways and means by which it was possible to make them aware of it. He was #emph[enraged] and struck them; and though perhaps this staggered them a little, still they went on. “He hides his face in anger” from them; he deserts them as to his gracious, helping, comforting presence. Does this have its effect? No, they still go on #emph[stubbornly], like men mad on their covetousness. Nothing can put a stop to their raging lusts. This is #emph[plain madness] and fury. We need not look far for instances; we see men mad on their lusts every day. And, which is the worst kind of #emph[madness], their lusts do not rage so much in them as they rage in pursuing them. Are those greedy pursuits of things in the world that we see some men engaged in, though they have other pretenses, really anything but plain #emph[madness] in pursuing their lusts? God, who searches the hearts of men, knows that most of the things done in the world under other pretenses are nothing but the actions of men mad and furious in pursuing their lusts.

Secondly, sin does not ordinarily rise to this height except when it has gained a double advantage.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, that it is provoked, enraged and heightened by some great #emph[temptation]. Though it is a poison in itself, yet because it is inborn in our nature, it does not grow violently outrageous unless #emph[Satan] contributes some new poison to it in a suitable temptation. It was the advantage that Satan gained against #emph[David] by a suitable temptation that raised his lusts to the #emph[rage] and #emph[madness] they went on to in the matter of #emph[Bathsheba] and #emph[Uriah]. Though sin is always a fire in the bones, it does not flame up unless Satan comes with his bellows to blow it up. And let anyone in whom the law of sin rises to this height of rage consider it seriously, and he may find out where the devil stands and puts his hand into the matter.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, it must be helped by some #emph[former] welcome and prevailing. Sin does not grow to this height at its first assault. If it had not been allowed to make its entrance, if there had not been some yielding in the soul, this would not have come about. The great wisdom and security of the soul in dealing with indwelling sin is to put a violent stop to its #emph[beginnings], its first motions and actions. Venture everything on the first attempt. Die rather than yield one step to it. Through the #emph[deceit] of sin, or the #emph[negligence] of the soul, or its carnal confidence that it can set bounds to the actions of lust at other times, sin may make some entrance into the soul and find some welcome. If it does, it gets strength and power, and rises imperceptibly to the frame we are considering. You would never have experienced the #emph[fury] of sin if you had not been content with some of its #emph[toyings]. If you had not brought up this servant, this slave, so pampered, it would not now presume to be more than a son. Now when the #emph[law of sin] in any particular has gained this double advantage, the help of a vigorous temptation and some prevailing gained before, by which it is let into the strongholds of the soul, it often rises up to the frame we are speaking of.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, we may see what #emph[goes with] this rage and madness, what its properties are, and what effects it produces.]

==== Casting Off the Rule of Grace

First, there is in it the casting off, for a time at least, of the yoke, rule and government of the Spirit and law of grace. Where grace has the #emph[dominion], it will never be utterly driven from its throne; it will still keep its right and sovereignty. But its #emph[influences] may be cut off for a time, and its government suspended, by the power of sin. Can we think that the #emph[law of grace] had any actual ruling influence on the heart of #emph[David] when, upon the provocation he received from #emph[Nabal], he was so carried away with the desire for revenge that he cried to his companions, “Strap on your swords!” and resolved not to leave one man of his whole household alive (1 Sam 25:34)? Or that #emph[Asa] was in any better frame when he “struck the prophet” who spoke to him in the name of the Lord and put him in prison? In this case sin is like an #emph[untamed horse], which, having first thrown its rider, runs away with fierceness and rage. It first throws off a present sense of the yoke of Christ and the law of his grace, and then drives the soul wherever it pleases. Let us consider a little how this is done.

The seat and residence of grace is in the whole soul. It is in the inner man; it is in the mind, the will, and the affections. For “the whole soul is renewed by it to the image of God” (Eph 4:23, 24), and the whole man is a “new creation” (2 Cor 5:17). And in all these it puts forth its power and efficacy. Its rule or dominion is the carrying on of its effectual working in all the faculties of the soul, as they are one united principle of moral and spiritual workings. So, then, the interrupting by the law of sin of the exercise of grace, of its rule and power, must consist in sin’s contrary action in and on the faculties and affections of the soul, on which and by which grace should put forth its power and efficacy. And this sin does. It #emph[darkens] the mind, partly through countless vain prejudices and false reasonings, as we will see when we come to consider its #emph[deceitfulness], and partly through the #emph[fumes] of the affections, heated with the foul lusts that have laid hold on them. So the saving light that is in the mind is clouded and stifled, so that it cannot put forth its #emph[transforming power] to change the soul into the likeness of Christ revealed to it, which is its proper work (Rom 12:2). The habitual inclination of the will to obedience, which is the next way the law of grace works, is first #emph[weakened], then cast aside and made useless by the continual pleadings of sin and temptation. So the will first lets go its hold and debates whether it will yield or not, and at last gives itself up to its adversary. And as for the #emph[affections], the beginning of this evil is commonly in them. They cross one another and torture the soul with their headlong violence. In this way the rule of the law of grace is cut off by the law of sin, which forces itself on the whole #emph[seat] of grace’s government. When this is done, sin will make sad work in the soul. The apostle warns believers to beware of this in Rom 6:12: “Therefore do not let sin reign in your#super[+] mortal body so that you#super[+] obey its lusts.” See to it that it does not get the #emph[dominion], that it does not usurp rule, not even for a moment. It will labor to push itself onto the #emph[throne]\; watch against it, or a woeful state and condition lies at the door. This, then, goes with this rage and madness of the law of sin: while it prevails, it wholly casts off the rule of the law of grace. The law of grace speaks in the soul, but is not heard; it commands the contrary, but is not obeyed. It cries out, “Do not do this detestable thing” that the Lord hates, but is not regarded. That is, it is not regarded so far as to be able to put a present stop to the rage of sin and recover its own rule, which God in his own time restores to it by the power of his Spirit dwelling in us.

==== Fearlessness and Contempt of Danger

Secondly, #emph[madness] or rage goes with #emph[fearlessness] and contempt of danger. It takes away the power of consideration, and all the influence consideration ought to have on the soul. That is why sinners who are wholly under the power of this rage are said “to rush at God, at the thick bosses of his shield” (Job 15:26), the very thing with which he is armed for their utter ruin. They despise the worst he can do to them, being secretly resolved to carry out their lusts even if it costs them their souls. A few considerations will make this clearer to us.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, often, when the soul has broken loose from the power of #emph[renewing grace], God deals with it to keep it within bounds by #emph[restraining grace]. So the Lord declares he will deal with #emph[Israel] (Hos 2:6), as if he said: Since you have rejected me, I will take another course with you. I will lay obstacles before you, so that you will not be able to go on where the fury of your lusts would drive you. He will set before them, from outside, what will obstruct them in their progress.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, these #emph[hindrances] that God lays in the way of sinners, as will be declared at length later, are of two sorts.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, #emph[rational considerations] taken from the consequences of the sin and evil the soul is being urged to and is perplexed with. Such are the #emph[fear of death], judgment, and hell, and of falling into the hands of the living God, who is a consuming fire. While a man is under the power of the law of the Spirit of life, “Christ’s love compels him” (2 Cor 5:14). The principle of his doing good and abstaining from evil is “faith, expressed through love,” together with following Christ because of the sweet fragrance of his name. But now, when this blessed “easy yoke” is thrown off for a time, as was shown before, God sets a hedge of #emph[terror] before the soul. He reminds it of death and the judgment to come, flashes the flames of hell-fire in its face, and fills the soul with the consideration of all the evil consequences of sin, to deter it from its purpose. To this end he makes use of all the threatenings recorded in the law and the gospel. Under this head also may be placed all the considerations that may be taken from temporal things, such as shame, reproach, scandal, punishments, and the like. By the consideration of these things, I say, God sets a #emph[hedge] before them.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, the Lord uses providential dealings to the same purpose, and these are of two sorts.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, such as are suited to work on the soul and cause it to stop and give up its lustings and pursuit of sin. Such are #emph[afflictions] and mercies. Isa 57:17: “I was enraged … so I struck him”; I showed my displeasure at their ways by afflictions. So also Hos 2:9, 11, 12. “God chastens men with pain” on their bodies, he says in #emph[Job], “to turn them from their purpose and to hide sin from them” (Job 33:17, 19). And he has other ways of coming to them and touching them, as in their names, relationships, estates and desirable things. Or else he heaps mercies on them, so that they may consider whom they are rebelling against. Perhaps signal, distinguishing mercies are made their portion for many days.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, such as #emph[actually hinder] the soul from pursuing sin, though it is resolved to do so. We must consider later the various ways in which God does #emph[this].]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[These, I say, are the ways in which the soul is dealt with after the law of #emph[indwelling sin] has thrown off for a time the influencing power of the law of grace. But now, when #emph[lust] rises up to #emph[rage] or #emph[madness], it will also despise all of these, even the rod and the One who ordained it. It will rush on shame, reproaches, wrath, and whatever may befall it. That is, though these are set before it, it will venture on them all. Rage and madness are #emph[fearless]. And it does this in two ways.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, it #emph[takes possession of the mind], so that it does not allow the consideration of these things to rest on it, but makes the thoughts of them slight and #emph[fleeting]. Or if the mind forces itself to consider them, sin still comes between the mind and the #emph[affections], so that they are not influenced by it in any proportion to what is required. The soul in such a condition will be able to consider such things and not be moved by them at all. And where they do prevail for a time, they are still imperceptibly worn off the heart again.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, by secret, stubborn #emph[resolutions] to venture everything on the way it is in.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And this is the second branch of this evidence of the #emph[power of sin], taken from the opposition it makes to the #emph[law of grace], #emph[as it were] by way of force, strength and violence. The consideration of its #emph[deceit] follows next.]]
